From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BDK Patches <patches@bdk.invalid>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:19:32 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] bdk: let the embedding application name its Keychain item

The key that protects cookies and passwords on macOS is kept in a Keychain
item named "Chromium Safe Storage". Every BDK application embeds the same
prebuilt runtime, so with a fixed name they would all share one item, and
renaming the browser would orphan its data. bdk.toml sets the name per
application ([storage] keychain_item, configuration.md section 3.2).

KeychainPassword already supports runtime names behind
ALLOW_RUNTIME_CONFIGURABLE_KEY_STORAGE (Electron uses it). Define it for
the keychain_password_mac target as a public config, so the target and every
file that includes its header agree on the types.

Used by: the BDK engine identity (//bdk/engine/identity.cc).
---
 components/os_crypt/common/BUILD.gn | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/components/os_crypt/common/BUILD.gn b/components/os_crypt/common/BUILD.gn
index 10db0687101b69542f9dd33592d0655c7343e3c8..d9d138c7031d090b8629f6899985d36b367e8eef 100644
--- a/components/os_crypt/common/BUILD.gn
+++ b/components/os_crypt/common/BUILD.gn
@@ -17,7 +17,15 @@ group("crypto_lib") {
 }
 
 if (is_apple) {
+  # BDK: each application that embeds the BDK runtime names its own Keychain
+  # item. The define makes the service and account names mutable; it is a
+  # public config because it changes the header's types for every includer.
+  config("keychain_password_mac_config") {
+    defines = [ "ALLOW_RUNTIME_CONFIGURABLE_KEY_STORAGE" ]
+  }
+
   source_set("keychain_password_mac") {
+    public_configs = [ ":keychain_password_mac_config" ]
     sources = [
       "keychain_password_mac.h",
       "keychain_password_mac.mm",
